1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:02,760
April sixteenth.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,440
Since the day the stall opened, Priya from

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,240
Infotech has taken chai for the whole floor,

4
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:09,280
every day.

5
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:10,700
On a tab.

6
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:12,280
Pay later.

7
00:00:12,831 --> 00:00:15,551
Today, Meera sends the first bill — two

8
00:00:15,631 --> 00:00:16,711
weeks of chai.

9
00:00:16,991 --> 00:00:18,511
Six thousand rupees.

10
00:00:18,751 --> 00:00:20,111
Her profit goes up.

11
00:00:20,431 --> 00:00:22,031
Her galla doesn't move.

12
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,080
So did Meera make money, or not?

13
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:34,467
First, last time's answers.

14
00:00:34,787 --> 00:00:35,307
One.

15
00:00:35,587 --> 00:00:37,147
Is the cart an expense?

16
00:00:37,427 --> 00:00:37,827
No.

17
00:00:37,987 --> 00:00:40,747
It's still making chai, so it's an asset.

18
00:00:41,527 --> 00:00:42,007
Two.

19
00:00:42,247 --> 00:00:45,127
Meera's profit: eighteen thousand of revenue,

20
00:00:45,207 --> 00:00:47,207
minus five thousand of rent.

21
00:00:47,247 --> 00:00:48,887
Thirteen thousand rupees.

22
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:50,287
Three.

23
00:00:50,487 --> 00:00:52,687
Profit lives on the right side of the scale,

24
00:00:52,927 --> 00:00:54,047
inside equity.

25
00:00:54,127 --> 00:00:55,407
It belongs to Meera.

26
00:00:56,473 --> 00:00:59,353
Today, Khata pins two needles on the stall's

27
00:00:59,513 --> 00:00:59,913
wall.

28
00:01:00,153 --> 00:01:01,593
The first is Profit.

29
00:01:01,993 --> 00:01:04,553
It moves when Meera earns money, or uses

30
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:05,193
it up.

31
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:08,893
The second is Cash — everything in the galla,

32
00:01:09,053 --> 00:01:10,573
plus everything in the bank.

33
00:01:11,031 --> 00:01:13,991
Right now, profit reads thirteen thousand.

34
00:01:14,191 --> 00:01:16,391
Cash reads fifty-one thousand.

35
00:01:16,471 --> 00:01:18,951
It's bigger, because it also holds Meera's

36
00:01:19,111 --> 00:01:20,711
savings and Ravi Mama's loan.

37
00:01:21,271 --> 00:01:23,031
Most people think these two needles move

38
00:01:23,111 --> 00:01:23,831
together.

39
00:01:24,071 --> 00:01:24,951
Let's watch.

40
00:01:26,002 --> 00:01:28,002
First, rewind one day.

41
00:01:28,242 --> 00:01:29,522
April fifteenth.

42
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:31,922
Meera carries fifteen thousand rupees from

43
00:01:32,002 --> 00:01:33,122
the galla to the bank.

44
00:01:33,522 --> 00:01:34,322
Profit?

45
00:01:34,402 --> 00:01:35,162
No change.

46
00:01:35,362 --> 00:01:36,042
Cash?

47
00:01:36,242 --> 00:01:37,322
Also no change.

48
00:01:38,022 --> 00:01:39,942
The money just moved from one pocket to

49
00:01:40,142 --> 00:01:40,662
another.

50
00:01:41,708 --> 00:01:43,748
Now, April sixteenth.

51
00:01:43,948 --> 00:01:45,068
Infotech's tab.

52
00:01:45,308 --> 00:01:46,988
Which two things changed?

53
00:01:48,958 --> 00:01:51,358
Sales went up by six thousand.

54
00:01:51,398 --> 00:01:53,598
The chai has been drunk, so Meera has earned

55
00:01:53,678 --> 00:01:54,238
it.

56
00:01:54,878 --> 00:01:57,318
But on the left, no cash came in.

57
00:01:57,558 --> 00:02:00,278
Instead, Infotech now owes the stall six

58
00:02:00,358 --> 00:02:01,238
thousand rupees.

59
00:02:01,718 --> 00:02:04,278
Money that customers owe you is an asset,

60
00:02:04,438 --> 00:02:05,798
called a receivable.

61
00:02:06,254 --> 00:02:08,654
So the profit needle jumps, from thirteen

62
00:02:08,734 --> 00:02:10,974
thousand to nineteen thousand.

63
00:02:11,054 --> 00:02:11,934
And the cash needle?

64
00:02:12,414 --> 00:02:13,934
It doesn't move at all.

65
00:02:15,542 --> 00:02:17,942
Revenue counts when it's earned — when the

66
00:02:18,102 --> 00:02:21,062
chai is drunk — not when the money arrives.

67
00:02:21,702 --> 00:02:23,822
And expenses count when they're incurred

68
00:02:24,142 --> 00:02:26,302
— when the thing is used up — not when they're

69
00:02:26,462 --> 00:02:27,102
paid.

70
00:02:27,882 --> 00:02:29,962
This idea is called accrual.

71
00:02:31,926 --> 00:02:33,446
April twentieth.

72
00:02:33,526 --> 00:02:35,926
Meera pays Gopal five thousand rupees of

73
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:38,406
the eight thousand she owes him for milk.

74
00:02:38,486 --> 00:02:40,246
Meera's sure about this one.

75
00:02:40,326 --> 00:02:42,966
Money left the galla, so it's an expense.

76
00:02:43,584 --> 00:02:44,144
Nope.

77
00:02:44,384 --> 00:02:46,944
That milk came in as stock, and Gopal became

78
00:02:46,984 --> 00:02:48,144
a liability.

79
00:02:48,304 --> 00:02:51,104
Today, Meera is only paying down that debt.

80
00:02:51,184 --> 00:02:52,544
So which needle moves?

81
00:02:54,514 --> 00:02:55,674
Only cash.

82
00:02:55,874 --> 00:02:57,394
Down five thousand.

83
00:02:57,594 --> 00:02:59,634
Profit stays at nineteen thousand.

84
00:03:00,214 --> 00:03:02,294
The payment shrinks a liability.

85
00:03:02,974 --> 00:03:05,374
Money the stall owes a supplier is a payable

86
00:03:05,774 --> 00:03:07,534
— the mirror of a receivable.

87
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:10,400
April twenty-second.

88
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:12,320
A customer pays four thousand rupees in

89
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:15,360
advance, for a catering order on May fifth.

90
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:17,220
Meera's quick this time.

91
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:18,660
Money in from a customer?

92
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:21,620
Revenue — straight into the profit pocket.

93
00:03:22,538 --> 00:03:23,058
Nope.

94
00:03:23,258 --> 00:03:25,378
Meera hasn't made a single cup yet.

95
00:03:25,578 --> 00:03:27,738
Until May fifth, she owes that customer

96
00:03:27,818 --> 00:03:30,138
an event — or their money back.

97
00:03:30,678 --> 00:03:33,278
An advance is a liability, not revenue.

98
00:03:33,598 --> 00:03:34,478
Not yet.

99
00:03:35,318 --> 00:03:37,078
Meera, which needle moves?

100
00:03:39,035 --> 00:03:40,115
Only cash.

101
00:03:40,315 --> 00:03:41,675
Up four thousand.

102
00:03:41,755 --> 00:03:43,995
Profit stays at nineteen thousand.

103
00:03:45,246 --> 00:03:47,086
Now you, on your own.

104
00:03:47,406 --> 00:03:48,926
April twenty-fifth.

105
00:03:49,046 --> 00:03:51,086
Infotech pays four thousand rupees of its

106
00:03:51,166 --> 00:03:53,046
tab, by UPI.

107
00:03:53,326 --> 00:03:54,046
Profit?

108
00:03:54,286 --> 00:03:55,006
Cash?

109
00:03:58,164 --> 00:04:00,564
Cash goes up by four thousand, straight

110
00:04:00,644 --> 00:04:01,604
into the bank.

111
00:04:01,804 --> 00:04:03,204
Profit doesn't move.

112
00:04:03,284 --> 00:04:06,164
Meera earned that money back on April sixteenth.

113
00:04:06,324 --> 00:04:08,404
Today, she's only collecting it.

114
00:04:08,944 --> 00:04:11,944
The receivable shrinks, and the bank grows.

115
00:04:13,208 --> 00:04:15,528
Since April fifteenth, the profit needle

116
00:04:15,608 --> 00:04:18,688
moved once — on the day no money came in.

117
00:04:19,568 --> 00:04:21,888
The cash needle moved three times — and

118
00:04:21,968 --> 00:04:23,808
not one of those was profit.

119
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:26,840
Profit: nineteen thousand rupees.

120
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:29,480
Cash: fifty-four thousand.

121
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:31,280
Two needles.

122
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:32,960
Two different stories.

123
00:04:33,728 --> 00:04:37,008
Cash basis counts money only when it moves.

124
00:04:37,628 --> 00:04:40,228
Accrual basis counts revenue when it's earned,

125
00:04:40,268 --> 00:04:42,468
and expenses when they're incurred.

126
00:04:43,248 --> 00:04:45,408
Businesses use accrual, because it shows

127
00:04:45,488 --> 00:04:47,568
what was really earned — even while the

128
00:04:47,648 --> 00:04:49,168
money is still on its way.

129
00:04:50,438 --> 00:04:52,118
So — two needles.

130
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:54,838
Earned isn't the same as received.

131
00:04:55,078 --> 00:04:57,398
Paid isn't the same as used up.

132
00:04:57,798 --> 00:04:59,878
Profit is not cash.

133
00:05:01,022 --> 00:05:01,702
Your turn.

134
00:05:01,902 --> 00:05:02,902
Three quick ones.

135
00:05:03,102 --> 00:05:03,582
One.

136
00:05:03,822 --> 00:05:05,742
The four-thousand-rupee catering advance

137
00:05:06,062 --> 00:05:07,422
— is it revenue in April?

138
00:05:07,902 --> 00:05:08,342
Two.

139
00:05:08,542 --> 00:05:10,942
Paying Gopal — is that an expense?

140
00:05:11,182 --> 00:05:11,742
Three.

141
00:05:11,982 --> 00:05:14,702
After the UPI payment, how much does Infotech

142
00:05:14,782 --> 00:05:15,822
still owe Meera?

143
00:05:16,062 --> 00:05:18,222
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

144
00:05:19,689 --> 00:05:21,563
And now — Checkpoint two.

145
00:05:21,803 --> 00:05:23,643
Back to Aman's samosa cart.

146
00:05:23,763 --> 00:05:24,923
Five things happen.

147
00:05:25,003 --> 00:05:27,083
For each one, write down what it does to

148
00:05:27,323 --> 00:05:29,083
profit, and to cash.

149
00:05:29,544 --> 00:05:30,144
One.

150
00:05:30,344 --> 00:05:32,904
Cash sales of nine thousand rupees.

151
00:05:33,064 --> 00:05:33,544
Two.

152
00:05:33,624 --> 00:05:36,344
He pays two thousand rupees of stall rent.

153
00:05:36,504 --> 00:05:36,984
Three.

154
00:05:37,064 --> 00:05:39,144
He supplies a school canteen with fifteen

155
00:05:39,224 --> 00:05:41,864
hundred rupees of samosas, on credit.

156
00:05:41,944 --> 00:05:42,584
Four.

157
00:05:42,664 --> 00:05:45,464
He pays Sharma Kirana two thousand rupees.

158
00:05:45,704 --> 00:05:46,264
Five.

159
00:05:46,344 --> 00:05:48,664
A customer gives him a thousand-rupee advance,

160
00:05:48,704 --> 00:05:50,584
for a birthday party next month.

161
00:05:50,704 --> 00:05:53,144
Pause the video now, and try all five.

162
00:05:56,307 --> 00:05:56,707
Ready?

163
00:05:57,267 --> 00:05:57,747
One.

164
00:05:57,987 --> 00:06:01,187
Profit and cash both go up nine thousand.

165
00:06:01,307 --> 00:06:01,507
Two.

166
00:06:01,907 --> 00:06:04,147
Both go down two thousand.

167
00:06:04,307 --> 00:06:04,867
Three.

168
00:06:05,067 --> 00:06:06,707
Profit up fifteen hundred.

169
00:06:06,907 --> 00:06:08,227
Cash, nothing.

170
00:06:08,307 --> 00:06:09,027
Four.

171
00:06:09,187 --> 00:06:10,707
Cash down two thousand.

172
00:06:10,947 --> 00:06:13,587
Profit, nothing — it's a debt being paid.

173
00:06:13,667 --> 00:06:14,467
Five.

174
00:06:14,627 --> 00:06:16,067
Cash up one thousand.

175
00:06:16,307 --> 00:06:18,627
Profit, nothing — it's an advance.

176
00:06:19,267 --> 00:06:21,627
Aman's profit so far: eight thousand five

177
00:06:21,707 --> 00:06:22,667
hundred rupees.

178
00:06:24,256 --> 00:06:26,936
Next time — remember that bank deposit?

179
00:06:27,216 --> 00:06:28,816
Meera's phone buzzed.

180
00:06:28,896 --> 00:06:31,296
Your account is credited with fifteen thousand

181
00:06:31,376 --> 00:06:32,176
rupees.

182
00:06:32,216 --> 00:06:33,296
Credited!

183
00:06:33,456 --> 00:06:34,976
Great news… right?
